I'm adding the best value to the team.

1246
01:01:25,040 --> 01:01:27,800
Instead of tying everyone up for an hour,

1247
01:01:27,800 --> 01:01:29,600
what if I just compile my data

1248
01:01:29,600 --> 01:01:31,640
into a brief memo for you to review?

1249
01:01:31,640 --> 01:01:32,580
Oh, that's brilliant.

1250
01:01:32,580 --> 01:01:34,960
You are saying no to the time-wisting event,

1251
01:01:34,960 --> 01:01:39,380
but you are framing it as a strategic collaborative decision.

1252
01:01:39,380 --> 01:01:41,740
You protect your time while sounding like a leader

1253
01:01:41,740 --> 01:01:44,020
who cares about team efficiency.

1254
01:01:44,020 --> 01:01:46,380
So what does this all mean when we bring it together?

1255
01:01:46,380 --> 01:01:48,900
The protocol is profoundly simple,

1256
01:01:48,900 --> 01:01:52,300
even if it requires fierce discipline to maintain.

1257
01:01:52,300 --> 01:01:55,700
Step one, you establish that morning margin of safety,

1258
01:01:55,700 --> 01:02:00,020
even if it's just 15 minutes, no screens, one intention.

1259
01:02:00,020 --> 01:02:02,740
Step two, you defend your deep focus at work

1260
01:02:02,740 --> 01:02:05,700
in short blocks using humanized mindset language

1261
01:02:05,700 --> 01:02:06,780
to communicate those boundaries.

1262
01:02:06,780 --> 01:02:07,620
Exactly.

1263
01:02:07,620 --> 01:02:10,960
Step three, you choose active tactile recovery

1264
01:02:10,960 --> 01:02:13,620
over passive digital numbing in the evenings

1265
01:02:13,620 --> 01:02:15,320
to stop emotional spillover.

1266
01:02:15,320 --> 01:02:17,900
And step four, you architect tomorrow

1267
01:02:17,900 --> 01:02:21,040
through subtraction, 10 minutes of preparation

1268
01:02:21,040 --> 01:02:22,700
and protecting your sleep.

1269
01:02:22,700 --> 01:02:24,360
And as you reflect on these mechanisms,

1270
01:02:24,360 --> 01:02:26,860
I wanna ask you, what stands out to you

1271
01:02:26,860 --> 01:02:29,300
about how your time is currently being spent?

1272
01:02:29,300 --> 01:02:31,820
Where are you leaking your most valuable capital

1273
01:02:31,820 --> 01:02:35,020
and what is one boundary you could experiment with tomorrow.

1274
01:02:35,020 --> 01:02:37,940
That really is the ultimate question.

1275
01:02:37,940 --> 01:02:40,460
The research notes that true active recovery

1276
01:02:40,460 --> 01:02:44,490
often looks incredibly boring to our overstimulated brains.

1277
01:02:44,490 --> 01:02:47,250
So tonight, when that familiar anxious urge

1278
01:02:47,250 --> 01:02:49,210
to pick up your phone hits,

1279
01:02:49,210 --> 01:02:51,530
what if you just sat with that uncomfortable boredom

1280
01:02:51,530 --> 01:02:54,690
for five minutes, observe what thoughts naturally bevel up

1281
01:02:54,690 --> 01:02:56,730
when the digital noise finally stops?

1282
01:02:56,730 --> 01:02:58,210
You might just find the exact solution

1283
01:02:58,210 --> 01:02:59,330
you've been searching for.

1284
01:02:59,330 --> 01:03:01,090
Don't leave the vault to your life wide open,

1285
01:03:01,090 --> 01:03:04,090
close the door, turn the lock, and take your time back.

1286
01:03:04,090 --> 01:03:08,050
Hello, everyone, and welcome back to Business English Conversations.

1287
01:03:08,050 --> 01:03:12,030
We're really happy to have you here with us today, and we're excited to bring you another

1288
01:03:12,030 --> 01:03:17,010
practical English lesson that you can actually use in real workplace situations.

1289
01:03:17,010 --> 01:03:18,370
Hi, everyone.

1290
01:03:18,370 --> 01:03:19,370
Welcome back.

1291
01:03:19,370 --> 01:03:22,170
Thank you so much for spending your time with us today.

1292
01:03:22,170 --> 01:03:25,530
I'm Daniel, and I'm here with my colleague, Sophia.

1293
01:03:25,530 --> 01:03:29,810
On this channel, we help you improve your English by practicing real conversations

1294
01:03:29,810 --> 01:03:34,690
that sound natural, professional, and useful for everyday work life.

1295
01:03:34,690 --> 01:03:35,690
That's right.

1296
01:03:35,690 --> 01:03:41,030
Today's topic is something very interesting and also very important in modern workplaces.

1297
01:03:41,030 --> 01:03:45,570
We're going to talk about how to explain your mindset in business meeting conversations.

1298
01:03:45,570 --> 01:03:49,010
Yes, and this is something people often forget.

1299
01:03:49,010 --> 01:03:54,270
In meetings, interviews, or performance discussions, it's not just about what you do.

1300
01:03:54,270 --> 01:03:59,770
It's also about how you think, how you approach problems, and how you handle challenges.

1301
01:03:59,770 --> 01:04:05,610
Exactly. Your mindset tells people a lot about you. For example, whether you are a problem

1302
01:04:05,610 --> 01:04:10,970
solver, whether you stay calm under pressure, whether you are open to feedback, or whether

1303
01:04:10,970 --> 01:04:13,210
you prefer structured thinking.

1304
01:04:13,210 --> 01:04:19,050
And many learners struggle with this because mindset feels abstract. It's not like describing

1305
01:04:19,050 --> 01:04:24,410
a project or a task. It's more about thoughts, attitudes, and behavior.

1306
01:04:24,410 --> 01:04:29,130
So today, we're going to show you how to talk about mindset in a natural way during

1307
01:04:29,130 --> 01:04:30,450
a business meeting.

1308
01:04:30,450 --> 01:04:35,050
We will use a realistic conversation between colleagues so you can understand how it works

1309
01:04:35,050 --> 01:04:36,250
in real life.

1310
01:04:36,250 --> 01:04:41,390
And we will include practical phrases you can actually use, not just theory.

1311
01:04:41,390 --> 01:04:43,010
So let's begin.

1312
01:04:43,010 --> 01:04:44,410
Imagine this situation.

1313
01:04:44,410 --> 01:04:48,670
You are in a team meeting and your manager asks you how you approach work challenges

1314
01:04:48,670 --> 01:04:50,170
and decision making.

1315
01:04:50,170 --> 01:04:51,890
I will start the conversation.

1316
01:04:51,890 --> 01:04:52,890
Great.

1317
01:04:52,890 --> 01:04:54,490
I'll respond as the colleague.

1318
01:04:54,490 --> 01:05:00,690
So before we move to the next agenda point, I'd like to ask something a bit more personal.

1319
01:05:00,690 --> 01:05:05,850
When you were handling a difficult task at work, how would you describe your mindset?

1320
01:05:05,850 --> 01:05:07,610
That's a really good question.

1321
01:05:07,610 --> 01:05:12,250
I think my mindset is quite focused on learning and problem solving.

1322
01:05:12,250 --> 01:05:17,290
When I face a challenge, I don't immediately think about whether I can or cannot do it.

1323
01:05:17,290 --> 01:05:20,930
I first try to understand the situation clearly.

1324
01:05:20,930 --> 01:05:22,050
That's interesting.

1325
01:05:22,050 --> 01:05:26,290
So you focus more on understanding rather than reacting emotionally.

1326
01:05:26,290 --> 01:05:28,050
Yes, exactly.

1327
01:05:28,050 --> 01:05:33,610
In the past, I used to react quickly and sometimes feel stressed when things were unclear.

1328
01:05:33,610 --> 01:05:39,530
But over time, I realized that staying calm and analyzing the situation gives better results.

1329
01:05:39,530 --> 01:05:43,530
Can you give an example from your work experience where this mindset helped you?

1330
01:05:43,530 --> 01:05:45,050
Yes, actually.

1331
01:05:45,050 --> 01:05:49,290
Recently, I was working on a project where we had a tight deadline and some missing

1332
01:05:49,290 --> 01:05:51,290
information from the client.

1333
01:05:51,290 --> 01:05:56,810
At first, I felt a bit pressured, but then I reminded myself to slow down and focus on

1334
01:05:56,810 --> 01:05:58,650
what I could control.

1335
01:05:58,650 --> 01:06:01,250
That sounds like a very practical approach.

1336
01:06:01,250 --> 01:06:02,370
What did you do next?

1337
01:06:02,370 --> 01:06:04,850
I broke the problem into smaller parts.

1338
01:06:04,850 --> 01:06:10,870
First, I listed what information we already had, then I identified what was missing.

1339
01:06:10,870 --> 01:06:15,770
After that, I contacted the client with specific questions, instead of sending a general

1340
01:06:15,770 --> 01:06:17,010
request.

1341
01:06:17,010 --> 01:06:21,290
That sounds very structured, so your mindset helped you stay organized.

1342
01:06:21,290 --> 01:06:22,850
Yes, exactly.

1343
01:06:22,850 --> 01:06:26,170
I think my mindset is very structured under pressure now.

1344
01:06:26,170 --> 01:06:27,490
I try not to panic.

1345
01:06:27,490 --> 01:06:32,410
Instead, I ask myself, what is the next small step I can take?

1346
01:06:32,410 --> 01:06:33,890
That's a powerful question.

1347
01:06:33,890 --> 01:06:35,450
I like that approach.

1348
01:06:35,450 --> 01:06:39,730
In meetings, people often overthink problems instead of breaking them down.

1349
01:06:39,730 --> 01:06:43,770
Yes, and I also learned that mindset is not something fixed.

1350
01:06:43,770 --> 01:06:45,450
It changes over time.

1351
01:06:45,450 --> 01:06:50,890
Earlier, I used to think that I needed to be perfect, now I think progress is more important

1352
01:06:50,890 --> 01:06:51,890
than perfection.

1353
01:06:51,890 --> 01:06:54,210
That's a very important shift.

1354
01:06:54,210 --> 01:06:56,450
So would you say you have a growth mindset?

1355
01:06:56,450 --> 01:06:58,290
Yes, I would say so.

1356
01:06:58,290 --> 01:07:01,450
I believe that skills can improve with practice.

1357
01:07:01,450 --> 01:07:05,370
Even if I don't know something today, I can learn it step by step.

1358
01:07:05,370 --> 01:07:06,370
That's great.

1359
01:07:06,370 --> 01:07:09,030
And how does this mindset affect your teamwork?

1360
01:07:09,030 --> 01:07:10,450
It helps a lot.

1361
01:07:10,450 --> 01:07:13,650
In team discussions, I'm more open to feedback now.

1362
01:07:13,650 --> 01:07:16,990
I don't feel defensive when someone suggests improvements.

1363
01:07:16,990 --> 01:07:20,610
Instead, I try to understand their perspective.

1364
01:07:20,610 --> 01:07:23,230
That's very useful in business environments.

1365
01:07:23,230 --> 01:07:25,050
Many people struggle with feedback.

1366
01:07:25,050 --> 01:07:27,450
Yes, I used to struggle too.

1367
01:07:27,450 --> 01:07:32,290
But now I see feedback as a tool for improvement, not as criticism.

1368
01:07:32,290 --> 01:07:36,770
For example, if someone says my report needs more clarity, I don't feel bad.

1369
01:07:36,770 --> 01:07:39,370
I ask, how can I make it clearer?

1370
01:07:39,370 --> 01:07:41,250
That's a very healthy mindset.

1371
01:07:41,250 --> 01:07:43,010
I want to ask something else.

1372
01:07:43,010 --> 01:07:46,130
How do you handle uncertainty in projects?

1373
01:07:46,130 --> 01:07:47,410
That's a good question.

1374
01:07:47,410 --> 01:07:51,330
I think my mindset towards uncertainty is acceptance.

1375
01:07:51,330 --> 01:07:55,530
I accept that not everything will be clear from the beginning.

1376
01:07:55,530 --> 01:07:59,890
Instead of waiting for perfect information, I start with what I have.

1377
01:07:59,890 --> 01:08:00,890
That's interesting.

1378
01:08:00,890 --> 01:08:03,730
So you are comfortable working with incomplete information?

1379
01:08:03,730 --> 01:08:05,570
Yes, to some extent.

1380
01:08:05,570 --> 01:08:10,450
Of course, I still need clarity for important decisions, but I don't wait for everything

1381
01:08:10,450 --> 01:08:12,930
to be perfect before starting.

1382
01:08:12,930 --> 01:08:14,490
It's very realistic.

1383
01:08:14,490 --> 01:08:17,450
In business, we rarely have perfect information.

1384
01:08:17,450 --> 01:08:18,570
Exactly.

1385
01:08:18,570 --> 01:08:21,730
And I think mindset plays a big role here.

1386
01:08:21,730 --> 01:08:24,930
If your mindset is too rigid, you get stuck.

1387
01:08:24,930 --> 01:08:29,150
But if your mindset is flexible, you can adapt more easily.

1388
01:08:29,150 --> 01:08:31,170
Flexibility is definitely important.

1389
01:08:31,170 --> 01:08:35,170
Can you explain how your mindset changes during stressful situations?

1390
01:08:35,170 --> 01:08:36,170
Yes.

1391
01:08:36,170 --> 01:08:39,410
During stress, my first reaction used to be emotional.

1392
01:08:39,410 --> 01:08:41,330
I would feel overwhelmed.

1393
01:08:41,330 --> 01:08:43,030
But now I try to pause.

1394
01:08:43,030 --> 01:08:45,730
I take a short moment before reacting.

1395
01:08:45,730 --> 01:08:48,770
I remind myself that stress is temporary.

1396
01:08:48,770 --> 01:08:50,930
That pause sounds very important.

1397
01:08:50,930 --> 01:08:51,930
It is.

1398
01:08:51,930 --> 01:08:55,410
Even five seconds of pause can change your thinking.

1399
01:08:55,410 --> 01:08:57,770
Instead of reacting, I respond.

1400
01:08:57,770 --> 01:09:00,770
That difference is very powerful in meetings.

1401
01:09:00,770 --> 01:09:02,410
Absolutely.

1402
01:09:02,410 --> 01:09:05,570
Responding instead of reacting is a great mindset shift.

1403
01:09:05,570 --> 01:09:09,970
Yes, and I also try to focus on solutions rather than problems.

1404
01:09:09,970 --> 01:09:14,970
When something goes wrong, I ask, what can we do now instead of why did this happen?

1405
01:09:14,970 --> 01:09:17,650
That's a very solution-oriented mindset.

1406
01:09:17,650 --> 01:09:20,770
Do you think this helps in leadership situations as well?

1407
01:09:20,770 --> 01:09:21,930
Definitely.

1408
01:09:21,930 --> 01:09:24,730
Leaders need to stay calm and guide others.

1409
01:09:24,730 --> 01:09:27,690
If a leader panics, the whole team feels it.

1410
01:09:27,690 --> 01:09:31,490
But if a leader stays focused, the team feels more confident.

1411
01:09:31,490 --> 01:09:33,090
That's very true.

1412
01:09:33,090 --> 01:09:37,490
Let's imagine you're in a meeting and someone asks you directly, how would you describe

1413
01:09:37,490 --> 01:09:41,010
your work mindset in one sentence, how would you answer?

1414
01:09:41,010 --> 01:09:47,250
I would say my mindset is focused on learning, staying calm under pressure, and finding practical

1415
01:09:47,250 --> 01:09:49,330
solutions step by step.

1416
01:09:49,330 --> 01:09:52,970
That's a very strong answer, clear and professional.

1417
01:09:52,970 --> 01:09:54,370
Let's go deeper now.

1418
01:09:54,370 --> 01:09:57,970
How do you stay motivated when work becomes repetitive?

1419
01:09:57,970 --> 01:09:59,610
That's a challenge sometimes.

1420
01:09:59,610 --> 01:10:02,530
I remind myself of the bigger purpose.

1421
01:10:02,530 --> 01:10:05,890
Even repetitive tasks contribute to the overall goal.

1422
01:10:05,890 --> 01:10:09,530
I try to connect small tasks with bigger outcomes.

1423
01:10:09,530 --> 01:10:11,570
That helps you see meaning in your work.

1424
01:10:11,570 --> 01:10:12,950
Yes, exactly.

1425
01:10:12,950 --> 01:10:16,610
If I only focus on repetition, I lose motivation.

1426
01:10:16,610 --> 01:10:20,330
But if I see the value, my mindset becomes more positive.

1427
01:10:20,330 --> 01:10:21,890
That's a great way to think.

1428
01:10:21,890 --> 01:10:23,970
I want to ask about mistakes.

1429
01:10:23,970 --> 01:10:26,730
How does your mindset handle mistakes at work?

1430
01:10:26,730 --> 01:10:29,410
I used to see mistakes as failures.

1431
01:10:29,410 --> 01:10:31,410
Now I see them as feedback.

1432
01:10:31,410 --> 01:10:35,090
When I make a mistake, I ask myself what I learned from it.

1433
01:10:35,090 --> 01:10:36,530
That's a big change.

1434
01:10:36,530 --> 01:10:39,010
Yes, and it reduces fear.

1435
01:10:39,010 --> 01:10:43,570
When you're not afraid of mistakes, you can try new things more confidently.

1436
01:10:43,570 --> 01:10:46,450
That's very important in business growth.

1437
01:10:46,450 --> 01:10:48,970
Innovation often comes from trial and error.

1438
01:10:48,970 --> 01:10:52,250
Exactly, and mindset is the foundation of that.

1439
01:10:52,250 --> 01:10:55,450
If your mindset is afraid, you will avoid risks.

1440
01:10:55,450 --> 01:10:59,170
If your mindset is open, you will explore more ideas.

1441
01:10:59,170 --> 01:11:01,050
Let's talk about communication.

1442
01:11:01,050 --> 01:11:04,410
How does mindset affect how you speak in meetings?

1443
01:11:04,410 --> 01:11:07,250
My mindset helps me listen more carefully.

1444
01:11:07,250 --> 01:11:08,850
I don't rush to speak.

1445
01:11:08,850 --> 01:11:11,330
I try to understand others first.

1446
01:11:11,330 --> 01:11:13,930
This makes my responses more thoughtful.

1447
01:11:13,930 --> 01:11:16,610
That's a very valuable communication skill.

1448
01:11:16,610 --> 01:11:21,250
Yes, and I also try to stay respectful even when I disagree.

1449
01:11:21,250 --> 01:11:25,770
Instead of saying, that's wrong, I might say, I see your point, but I think we

1450
01:11:25,770 --> 01:11:28,530
could also consider another option.

1451
01:11:28,530 --> 01:11:29,850
That's a great phrase.

1452
01:11:29,850 --> 01:11:32,050
It sounds professional and balanced.

1453
01:11:32,050 --> 01:11:33,050
Thank you.

1454
01:11:33,050 --> 01:11:35,990
I think mindset influences language a lot.

1455
01:11:35,990 --> 01:11:39,550
If your mindset is calm, your words are calm too.

1456
01:11:39,550 --> 01:11:40,550
That's very true.

1457
01:11:40,550 --> 01:11:43,230
Now, let's talk about decision-making.

1458
01:11:43,230 --> 01:11:45,710
How does your mindset affect decisions?

1459
01:11:45,710 --> 01:11:47,910
I try not to rush decisions.

1460
01:11:47,910 --> 01:11:51,230
I collect information first, then reflect.

1461
01:11:51,230 --> 01:11:54,790
I ask myself, is this aligned with our goals?

1462
01:11:54,790 --> 01:11:57,150
And what are the possible outcomes?

1463
01:11:57,150 --> 01:11:58,630
That sounds very logical.

1464
01:11:58,630 --> 01:12:02,230
Yes, but I also trust intuition when needed.

1465
01:12:02,230 --> 01:12:06,310
I think good mindset is a balance between logic and intuition.

1466
01:12:06,310 --> 01:12:09,110
That's a very mature perspective.

1467
01:12:09,110 --> 01:12:14,310
Let's imagine a junior colleague asks you, how can I improve my mindset at work?

1468
01:12:14,310 --> 01:12:15,310
What would you say?

1469
01:12:15,310 --> 01:12:17,550
I would say, start small.

1470
01:12:17,550 --> 01:12:20,230
Don't try to change everything at once.

1471
01:12:20,230 --> 01:12:25,810
Focus on one habit, like staying calm during one meeting or asking more questions instead

1472
01:12:25,810 --> 01:12:27,590
of reacting quickly.

1473
01:12:27,590 --> 01:12:29,390
That's very practical advice.

1474
01:12:29,390 --> 01:12:33,470
Yes, and I would also say, be kind to yourself.

1475
01:12:33,470 --> 01:12:35,150
Mindset improvement takes time.

1476
01:12:35,150 --> 01:12:36,630
It's not instant.

1477
01:12:36,630 --> 01:12:38,350
That's an important reminder.

1478
01:12:38,350 --> 01:12:40,550
Many people expect fast results.

1479
01:12:40,550 --> 01:12:41,550
Exactly.

1480
01:12:41,550 --> 01:12:45,310
But mindset is built daily through small choices.

1481
01:12:45,310 --> 01:12:47,530
Before we finish, let's summarize.

1482
01:12:47,530 --> 01:12:51,470
What are the key points when talking about mindset in business meetings?

1483
01:12:51,470 --> 01:12:54,830
First, describe how you think during challenges.

1484
01:12:54,830 --> 01:12:57,870
Second, give real examples from your work.

1485
01:12:57,870 --> 01:13:03,530
Third, explain how your mindset helps teamwork, communication, and decision-making.

1486
01:13:03,530 --> 01:13:07,270
And finally, show that you're open to learning and improvement.

1487
01:13:07,270 --> 01:13:09,030
That's a very clear structure.

1488
01:13:09,030 --> 01:13:11,790
It helps listeners understand your thinking process.

1489
01:13:11,790 --> 01:13:15,990
Yes, and it makes your answers sound more professional and confident.

1490
01:13:15,990 --> 01:13:18,110
Thank you everyone for listening today.

1491
01:13:18,110 --> 01:13:22,190
We hope this conversation helps you feel more confident when talking about your mindset

1492
01:13:22,190 --> 01:13:23,710
in business situations.

1493
01:13:23,710 --> 01:13:27,670
Yes, remember that mindset is not about being perfect.

1494
01:13:27,670 --> 01:13:31,590
It's about how you respond, adapt, and grow over time.

1495
01:13:31,590 --> 01:13:35,230
Keep practicing these ideas in your daily English conversations.

